I recall reading at one point an in-depth list of what kinds of wishes
would be useful in real life. I think it was in rgrn?

Boots of speed is tempting, but it depends on the side effects - does
it boost my mental faculties accordingly? Do I need to sleep twice as
often? Etc. In addition, perceiving things in slow motion would be
socially awkward, but I guess this would be alleviated by selectively
not wearing them. I guess another problem is that such an ability in
action would be too attention garnering - if you're picking a wish,
you either want it to be discrete, or so powerful such that knowledge
of its existence won't give you problems. A unicorn horn would be
great, but how do you stop it being stolen from you?

So, you want something that's inconspicuous yet generally useful to
have. Your first wish, you might want to debate about - is this wand
of wishing fresh or recharged? Or am I wresting? If you think it's
fresh, 2 blessed scrolls of charging. You may not want to take the
risk, though, and you're still guaranteed at least one wish even
without a charge - maybe that's all you want. Also, all wishes should
be blessed fixed +1 (and possibly greased too), and for two of it if
you can have multiples. (I say +1 and 2x instead of the statistically
better +2 and 3x because of the concept of utility; the difference
between 1x and 2x is more useful than the difference between 2x and
3x. Same for +0 -> +1 and +1 -> +2. With only limited wishes and no
guaranteed way of getting more, you want to maximize utility.)

Amulets: Life saving of course. Magical breathing is a bit more
specific in use, but it sounds like it would be great for a swimmer or
mountain climber, say. Make sure you're find with the hunger cost,
though.

Food: If you're blind or know someone who is, a carrot. Assuming it
doesn't only help with temporary blindness - it's a bit of a gamble.
If you're a biologist or know someone who is, it'd be fun to make a
foo egg and do experiments on it, or see if it has DNA or whathaveyou.
Tins: Mind flayer for intelligence/telepathy, giant for strength.
Floating eye for telepathy. Quantum mechanic for intrinsic speed, but
not being able to toggle it would turn on you in the end. Foo dragon
meat, if you ever feel the urge; most don't seem generally useful, but
sleep resistance would be great for night shifts and the like, or
perhaps fire/cold if you live some place with a rough climate
(assuming fire helps with heat?). If you're a gambler, tengu.

Gems: Blessed luckstone sounds like a good idea.

Armour: I thought about dragon scale mail, but wouldn't it get
tiresome to wear it everywhere? Actually, at only 40 weight it might
not, but it'd still be quite suspicious - and most everyday situations
don't require some kind of esoteric resistance, anyway. You could wish
up a t-shirt to get it blessed, fixed and +1, but then you'd have to
wear it all the time, again. The cloaks of displacement, magic
resistance and protection similarly aren't generally useful, but the
cloak of invisibility would be great for all sorts of things. In a
similar vein, wearing a cornuthaum or helm of brilliance everywhere
would look dorky. Gauntlets of dexterity and power sound like good
choices, and are more fashionally justifiable. The boots are varied
and rather interesting: elven, kicking (for sports, perhaps?),
levitation, jumping (same), speed, water walking. Any of them sound
like they'd be nifty for outdoors adventures, or base jumping or
whathaveyou. Just don't get people asking questions.

Misc: A statue of a foo can be sold as a piece of art.

Artifacts: Lots of ones to think about here - problem is the zapping
they can give you. Are you -sure- you can survive it? You've never
adventured, after all. Swords are most likely out of the question, but
the orb of detection, magic mirror of merlin, staff of aesculapius,
eyes of the overworld, platinum yendorian express card, orb of fate,
eye of the aethiopica and master key of thievery all have varying
usefulness. The staff particularly sounds great to have around; the
card, too, if it comes with a good account. Also, if your artifact
wish fails, you'll have useful (if troubling) information: Multiple
artifacts already exist in the world.
(Does enlightenment show you whether you're lawful, chaotic or
neutral? Or does the helm of opposite alignment tell you? It might be
useful for making sure if you can take an artifact without suffering
or not.)

Potions: Enlightenment, full healing, gain ability, gain energy, gain
level and see invisiblity all have useful, permanent effects. Gain
ability in particular would be amazing. If, somehow, you know which
potion is smoky, you could go for it in hopes of getting more wishes -
you will however have a significant chance of not surviving the
attempt!

Rings: Make sure you don't mind the extra hunger cost, if you intend
on relying upon one of these. Rings are a good choice in general -
they're small and inconspicuous, and have many nice effects. Teleport
+ teleport control, for instance, or polymorph + polymorph control
(don't put them on in the wrong order!). Invisibility, slow digestion,
free action, warning, levitation and so on. Sustain ability in
particular would be great to ensure your physical and mental faculties
never go to waste.

Scrolls: Not many of permanent use; charging has an obvious use, but
aside from that, what else? Taming? Genocide? Enchant armour?
Identify? Magic mapping?

Tools: Bag of holding has ridiculous use, obviously - but you'd have
to be careful to ensure people didn't catch on. Unicorn horn, too,
would be very suspicious - perhaps keep it locked away, and use it
only personally. Hope you don't get pangs of guilt from that one.
Stethoscope might be useful - might. Tinning kit is useless without
magical monsters to tin. Figurine sounds great, but would you really
risk a 1 in 10 chance of dieing? Magic marker sounds useful, but what
would you write with it?

Wands: Aside from maybe enlightenment, not much of use I'm seeing
here. The explicitly temporal nature of such a wish really kills it.
Perhaps some wise uses of make invisible, plus something to let you
see invisible - that would stick with you forever. Or you could
experiment with polymorph, but that might not get you anything good.

I've left out lots of wishes that would be purely malicious - a wand
of fire or slow monster, for instance. There doesn't seem much use in
such an investment, but maybe I'm not thinking creatively enough - you
could combine it with invisibility, say. Or even conflict. Is there
something that would protect against bullets and explosions? Teleport
and teleport control?


